Z||000000||-=shame=-||11-24-2000||10:20 AM||||Ok. First off, I know that the guy that had this before me was having problems because of the beautiful matching wipers, and the only clean part under the hood was the wiper assembly. Now, No washer fluid ever. The wipers are really making me mad, hanging up in the middle of windsheild. Plus one cold morning I broke the stalk trying to turn the mist cycle on. Plastic and cold BAD!||||reg||1
Z||000001||deceler8||11-24-2000||10:30 AM||||One of the funniest things I've ever seen (and ingenious too) was on a crappy, rusted out mid 70's Accord...<P>This guy was driving in a rainstorm on the interstate and his passenger was operating the wipers with a rope that went through the drivers and passengers window.<P>I remember laughing about it and then thinking..."Man, that's genius"...||||reg||1
Z||000002||StuGood||11-24-2000||04:14 PM||||Putting the 2 previous posts together, I get the following:<P>If wipers fail, run ropes from the wipers into the passenger compartment, and operate the wipers manually.  But if it's cold, don't use a nylon (plastic) rope.||||reg||12
Z||000003||theogre||11-24-2000||05:26 PM||||sounds like a bad wiper motor. I thought someone around here said the ones on some of the blazers was the same motor.....||||reg||1
Z||000004||robertjay||11-25-2000||10:31 AM||||I had the same problem on my 95 Chevy truck.  I took the motor assembly apart and cleaned and lubed the rotating contacts that tell the motor where to "park".  It's worked fine ever since.||||reg||1
